module examples.layout_wire

# plans/M7.md item B: `@layout(wire)` — persistent/network bytes, "exact
# encoding independent of any target" (03-hardware.md §3). Two things this
# case exists to pin that the `dma`/`mmio` cases cannot:
#
#   - `endian=big`. The wire kind is the one whose bytes leave the machine,
#     so network byte order is a real declaration, not a curiosity; the
#     docs never enumerate the allowed values, and this compiler accepts
#     exactly `little` and `big` (plans/M7.md item B, reported as a plan
#     decision).
#   - An explicit `@offset` that skips ahead. The 12 bytes between
#     `blocks` and `checksum` are *declared* padding — the author wrote the
#     hole — so they are reported, not rejected. The padding a `@layout`
#     rejects is the padding the compiler would otherwise invent
#     (`err-layout-implicit-padding`).
#
# Decoding these bytes (`Bytes.read_wire[W]`, 03 §3 / 05-library.md §9) is
# not implemented and is not needed to read a block; it fails closed by
# name until the item that needs it.

@layout(wire, endian=big)
struct Inode:
    magic: u32
    size_bytes: u32
    start_lba: u64
    blocks: u32
    @offset(0x20) checksum: u32
